add('N074','I. Algebraic groups, torsion, and the norm argument','The five conditions and their incomplete right-hand sides',[(47,3),(47,12),(50,3)],'reading question',r'“Conditions équivalentes”; “Nous prouverons”; “c′ … b′”',r'alg[ée]brique|torus|tore|finite|cohomolog|class.two',r'''The proposition lists \(a,b,b',c,c'\), with an empty right side in \(c\), a cancelled equality in \(c'\), and a cancelled implication in the diagram; its proof announcement names \(c\Rightarrow b'\), while scan 050 begins with an uncertain \(c'\) or \(c\). Which exact vanishing condition on the unit group of a separable algebra is used by the subsequent group-algebra and norm maps?''',r'''The context supplies no version of this five-condition proposition; LM7_PUSH C.6 concerns a claimed class-two residue classification, and no map from its residue complex to these algebraic-group coefficient groups is supplied.''',['LM7_PUSH C.6'])
add('N075','I. Algebraic groups, torsion, and the norm argument','The finite-module dévissage actually given',[(47,24)],'unpursued implication',r'“puis en filtrant”; “puis enfin au cas irréductible”',r'filtr|irréduct|irreduc|torsion|class.two|residue',r'''The proof of \(b'\Rightarrow b\) gives the filtration \(\Gamma\supset\ell\Gamma\supset\ell^2\Gamma\supset\cdots\), followed by a composition-series step for finite \(\mathbf F_\ell\)-representations. Its relevant maps are the inclusions and quotient maps of that filtration and the resulting cohomology exact sequences; this is a written proof mechanism available for comparison with the more abbreviated reductions elsewhere in the package.''',r'''LM7_PUSH C.6 has Proved status only as its return’s claim and concerns a different specified residue complex; the supplied excerpt gives no morphism identifying its filtration with this one, so its claimed classification is neither repeated nor extended here.''',['LM7_PUSH C.6'])
add('N076','I. Algebraic groups, torsion, and the norm argument','The decomposition explicitly marked for verification',[(47,31),(48,3)],'self-flagged',r'“G=G_u×G_s”; “définie sur k”; “à vérifier”',r'unipot|semisimple|semi.simple|parfait|perfect|algebraic group',r'''The margin marks the decomposition and its definition over \(k\) for verification, within the proof’s perfect-field branch begun on scan 047. Milne, Algebraic Groups, Theorem 16.13 gives the corresponding multiplication isomorphism \(G_u\times G_s\to G\) for a commutative affine algebraic group over a perfect field [P13]; the manuscript’s prior reduction to the linear case is the scope to retain in that comparison.''',r'''No supplied context excerpt verifies this decomposition; the scan adds a source-marked structural step within the general cohomological-dimension programme.''',[],['P13'],flags=['self'])
add('N077','I. Algebraic groups, torsion, and the norm argument','The degree-one boundary for torsion coefficients',[(48,11),(48,15)],'unproved claim',r'“pour i≥1”; “G … G/U est une … surjection”',r'torsion|divisib|H.?1|cohomolog|norm',r'''The source passes from higher vanishing of \(G/U\) to \(H^i(\mathfrak g,G)\simeq H^i(\mathfrak g,U)\) for \(i\geq1\), citing surjectivity of \(G\to G/U\). In degree one the exact sequence also contains \(G^{\mathfrak g}\to(G/U)^{\mathfrak g}\to H^1(\mathfrak g,U)\), so which argument gives surjectivity on these invariants rather than only on the underlying groups?''',r'''The supplied context has no such coefficient comparison; N089 supplies a concrete cocycle for the related question on scan 051, connecting two places within this package through the same inclusion \(U\hookrightarrow G\).''',[])
add('N078','I. Algebraic groups, torsion, and the norm argument','The essential filtered limit and the requested lemma',[(48,7),(48,18),(49,3)],'self-flagged',r'“dire qqp”; “limite inductive est essentiel”; “dégager un lemme”',r'limite inductive|direct limit|torsion|finite|cohomolog',r'''The margin asks to extract a lemma and stresses that passing to a filtered limit is essential before using the finite groups \(U(m)\). The map requiring a proof is \(\varinjlim_m H^i(\mathfrak g,U(m))\to H^i(\mathfrak g,\bigcup_mU(m))\), with continuous cochains and the topology on the Galois module made explicit.''',r'''LM7 E.23 and INTAKE LM7_CONTINUED §? involve continuous section maps and a convergence condition, but their excerpted correction modules are not identified with this torsion union; these pages add a method question with a precise direct-limit map.''',['LM7 E.23','INTAKE LM7_CONTINUED §?'],flags=['self'])
add('N079','I. Algebraic groups, torsion, and the norm argument','The positive-characteristic vector-group descent step',[(49,8),(49,11),(49,13)],'unproved claim',r'“donc … aussi sur k (car H^1(k,Gl(n))=0)”',r'unipot|Gl|GL|descent|descente|parfait|perfect',r'''The page invokes \(H^1(k,\operatorname{GL}_n)=0\) to descend a vector-group description in both characteristic zero and the exponent-\(p\) step. Milne’s Proposition 14.54 treats the latter through \(P(G)=\operatorname{Hom}(G,\mathbf G_a)\) as a skew-polynomial module [P13]; which chosen linear structure and Galois cocycle place the manuscript’s descent datum in \(\operatorname{GL}_n\)?''',r'''MPL_131 RN001’s descent lead (131/002,017) and the explicit GL(n) question on this package’s scan 044 give comparison points, but the supplied context contains no descent datum for these unipotent groups.''',['MPL_131 RN001'],['P13'])
add('N080','I. Algebraic groups, torsion, and the norm argument','An additive automorphism to test the chosen linear structure',[(49,13)],'own idea',r'“car H^1(k,Gl(n))=0”',r'Gl|GL|unipot|descent|descente|Frobenius',r'''Over \(\mathbf F_p\), the map \(a:\mathbf G_a^2\to\mathbf G_a^2\), \((x,y)\mapsto(x+y^p,y)\), is an additive group automorphism with inverse \((x,y)\mapsto(x-y^p,y)\), because \((y+z)^p=y^p+z^p\). As a morphism of group schemes it cannot be represented by a constant \(2\times2\) matrix: its first coordinate contains the degree-\(p\) monomial \(y^p\), which has no expression as a degree-one polynomial in \(\mathbf F_p[x,y]\). Which linear structure does the manuscript choose so that its particular descent cocycle avoids this obstruction to a direct GL(n) argument?''',r'''This sharpens the descent-method link to MPL_131 RN001 and the question on scan 044, while the context supplies no such choice of linear structure.''',['MPL_131 RN001'],confidence='high for the explicit group-scheme automorphism; unresolved for the manuscript’s implicit choice',reason='Both inverse and additivity are polynomial identities, and equality with a matrix morphism would require equality of polynomials of different degrees.')
add('N081','I. Algebraic groups, torsion, and the norm argument','The arbitrary-field branch left unwritten',[(49,15)],'unproved claim',r'“Supposons que k soit quelconque …”',r'parfait|perfect|arbitrary|quelconque|radiciel|descent',r'''The proof stops immediately after announcing the arbitrary-field case, following a branch that used perfection and descent over \(\bar k\). The missing comparison must specify the base extension, the induced maps on algebraic groups and their cohomology, and the handling of the unipotent subgroup whose definition over \(k\) was used earlier.''',r'''No supplied context entry claims this missing branch; the package therefore adds an unfinished proof boundary rather than evidence that the full five-condition proposition has been established on these leaves.''',[])
add('N082','I. Algebraic groups, torsion, and the norm argument','The C2 regular norm tests the excluded case',[(50,6),(50,7)],'own idea',r'“on vérifie facilement que ce deuxième cas est exclu”',r'norm|irreduc|irréduct|Γ|Gamma|torsion',r'''Take \(k=\mathbf C\) and \(\Gamma=C_2=\{1,s\}\), the constant one-dimensional \(\mathbf F_2\)-module for the trivial absolute Galois group, so its nonzero module is irreducible. In \(A=\mathbf C[C_2]\), multiplication by \(s\) in the basis \((1,s)\) has matrix \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\1&0\end{smallmatrix}\right)\), determinant \(-1\), and hence \(\Gamma\cap\ker N=\{1\}\). Does this concrete instance expose an omitted treatment of the \(\ell=2\), one-dimensional case in the sentence excluding it?''',r'''The context’s norm leads MPL_60_036-070 §2 and MPL_11_043-058+56 N045 give no regular group-algebra norm calculation; this item tests the present proof step only, with no novelty claim or conclusion about the whole proposition.''',['MPL_60_036-070 §2','MPL_11_043-058+56 N045'],confidence='high for the explicit determinant and module',reason='The norm is the determinant specified by the source, the matrix is written out, and the constant F2-module has no proper nonzero submodule.')
add('N083','I. Algebraic groups, torsion, and the norm argument','An extra factor as a construction to examine',[(50,6)],'own idea',r'“noyau de l’homomorphisme N”',r'norm|algebra|algèbre|torsion|represent',r'''In the concrete algebra \(B=\mathbf C[C_2]\times\mathbf C\), multiplication by \((a,z)\) has block matrix \(\operatorname{diag}(L_a,z)\), so its norm is \(N_A(a)z\). The explicit embedding \(j(1)=(1,1)\), \(j(s)=(s,-1)\) has \(j(s)^2=(1,1)\) and norm one at both elements. Can this extra-factor construction repair the specific embedding step in N082, and what change would it require in the draft’s following dimension and torsion-kernel calculation?''',r'''No supplied context excerpt gives this embedding; the question is a proposed continuation of the current norm calculation and does not assert completion of the original proof.''',[],confidence='high for this concrete norm-one embedding; unresolved for continuation of the draft',reason='The block determinant and injective first coordinate verify the construction, while the remainder of the argument is only a short unfinished tail.')
add('N084','I. Algebraic groups, torsion, and the norm argument','The final dimension symbol and unfinished induction',[(50,9)],'reading question',r'“n_1=n-1”; “n l’ordre de \unsure{G|Γ}”',r'dimension|ordre|order|norm|torsion|induct',r'''The final line uses \(n\) for an order in the group-algebra dimension calculation after the proposition had used \(n\) as its cohomological threshold, and its last group letter remains uncertain. Which separate integers govern the dimension of the norm-one torus, the order \(|\Gamma|\), and the cohomological degree, and what exact sequence follows the inclusion into the \(\ell\)-torsion kernel?''',r'''The supplied context has no continuation of this tail; the ambiguity and the absence of the next exact sequence must remain visible when assessing the proposition or comparing any earlier norm lead.''',[])

